4. Your hreflang system is clever, but has one potential problem

This works beautifully for pages that exist in both languages.

For:

/about/

it generates:

EN → /about/
DE → /de/about/

Excellent.

For:

/de/about/

it generates the same reciprocal pair.

Also excellent.

But here's the problem

It assumes every English page has a German equivalent and vice versa.

Suppose you later create:

/blog/best-adhd-planning-apps/

but don't translate it.

Your code will nevertheless output:

<link rel="alternate"
      hreflang="de"
      href="https://www.arianemader.com/de/blog/best-adhd-planning-apps/" />

even if that page doesn't exist.

That's bad.

For your current website, because you have corresponding EN/DE versions of your main pages, this is probably fine.

But when we create the content/blog section we discussed, I'd change this architecture so alternate URLs are explicitly supplied when a translation actually exists.